new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8f7b278
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+# Deploy Fizzy to a single server
+
+Fizzy and its Solid Queue workers run in one Docker app container. Kamal Proxy
+handles public HTTPS with Let's Encrypt and forwards traffic to the app over
+HTTP. No separate database or job server is needed.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- A Linux server with Docker running and an SSH user allowed to run Docker.
+  Kamal connects as `root` unless you set `ssh.user`. SSH must allow remote port
+  forwarding for Kamal's local image registry.
+- A public DNS name pointing to the server. Ports 80 and 443 must be reachable
+  from the internet and available for Kamal Proxy.
+- An SMTP server reachable from the Linux server. Fizzy sends sign-in and sign-up
+  codes by email.
+- On your deploy machine: Docker with Buildx and Kamal 2.11 or later. From your
+  fork's checkout, use `bundle install` and `bin/kamal` for the bundled version,
+  or install Kamal separately and use `kamal`. Kamal starts its own local
+  registry, so you don't need a registry account.
+
+## Set up and deploy
+
+1. Edit `config/deploy.yml`. Replace `fizzy.example.com` under `servers.web` with
+   your public hostname (no `https://`). The proxy and Fizzy use the same name.
+   Set `builder.arch` to `arm64` if your server is ARM.
+2. In the same file, set `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`
+   to the settings for your mail provider. The defaults use STARTTLS on port 587.
+   For implicit TLS, set `SMTP_PORT: "465"` and add `SMTP_TLS: "true"` under
+   `env.clear`. For a trusted, unauthenticated relay that doesn't support TLS,
+   set its port and add `SMTP_STARTTLS: "false"`. Set `SMTP_DOMAIN` only if your
+   relay requires a different SMTP greeting domain from your public hostname.
+3. Create your local secrets file:
+
+   ```sh
+   cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+   chmod 600 .kamal/secrets
+   openssl rand -hex 64
+   ```
+
+   Put the generated value in `.kamal/secrets` as `SECRET_KEY_BASE`. Set
+   `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD` there, or leave both blank if your relay
+   doesn't require authentication. Keep the file private and reuse the same key
+   across deploys.
+4. Run setup from the checkout. Kamal reads your local deployment settings and
+   builds the app from the current Git commit, so commit any app code changes first.
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/kamal setup
+   ```
+
+   Open `https://your-host/signup` to create the first account and verify that
+   you receive a sign-up code. Use `bin/kamal deploy` for later updates.
+
+For optional browser push notifications, [generate VAPID keys](development.md#web-push-notifications),
+add both names to `env.secret` in `config/deploy.yml`, and put the values in
+`.kamal/secrets`.
+
+## Persistent data
+
+Kamal mounts the Docker volume `fizzy_storage` at `/rails/storage`. It holds the
+SQLite databases (including queued jobs) and uploaded files, and is reused when
+Kamal replaces the app container. Back up the volume and your secrets. If you
+move to a new server, restore the volume before starting Fizzy there.
